      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Jack L. Davis's paper \"The Lord of the Gold Rings: 
The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" reports the 2015 discovery of the Griffin 
Warrior's Late Helladic IIA tomb near Tholos IV at the Palace of Nestor at 
Pylos. Hundreds of artifacts were recovered, including four gold signet rings 
with Minoan ritual iconography. The paper notes that some grave goods closely 
parallel finds from other western Messenian burials. In discussing the gold 
rings and broader corpus of signet rings, it compares bands and iconography to 
well-known rings such as the \"Ring of Nestor\" and the \"Ring of Minos,\" 
emphasizing similarities in bands and find contexts and situating early 
Mycenaean rings within broader Aegean ritual and funerary practices.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The Scribd document \"Princes of Pylos\" (Treasures 
of Mycenaean Pylos) includes an illustrated catalogue of artifacts associated 
with Pylos. One figure caption identifies a gold signet ring as the \"Ring of 
Nestor.\" The caption states that the ring is Minoan in date, around 1500 BCE, 
specifies its gold material and dimensions, and locates it in the Ashmolean 
Museum with an accession number. This contextualizes the Ring of Nestor as a 
Minoan-period gold signet ring, not a Mycenaean grave find from Pylos, and 
associates it instead with museum collections.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "A Facebook post from \"Archaeology and Ancient 
World\" describes the \"Ring of Nestor\" as a 3700-year-old gold signet ring 
from Crete dating to the Late Minoan period. It states that the authenticity of 
the Ring of Nestor is disputed and that it was first described by archaeologist 
Arthur Evans in 1925. The post notes the ring's detailed design and suggests 
that its features contribute to debates about whether it is an authentic Bronze 
Age artifact or a later forgery.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Academia.edu paper \"Some observations on the 
iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor'\" analyzes the imagery of the Ring of Nestor
and situates it within discussions of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates.
It treats the ring as a case study in \"gemmae dubitandae\"—objects of doubtful 
authenticity—examining its scenes (including female figures, boats, altars, 
sacred trees, and baetyls) and how these correspond to or diverge from genuine 
Minoan and Mycenaean iconographic traditions. The paper contributes to the 
scholarly argument that the Ring of Nestor's archaeological context is 
problematic or unknown and that its authenticity remains contested.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The chapter \"Fakes and Dubitanda\" in the Cambridge 
volume \"The Art and Archaeology of the Aegean Bronze Age\" discusses 
problematic or possibly forged artifacts. It mentions the Ring of Nestor 
alongside other objects, noting that like the Ring of Nestor, another ring 
displays a combination of scenes (a female individual on a boat, altars, sacred 
trees, and baetyls) tied to themes of divinity. The context suggests that the 
Ring of Nestor is considered among artifacts whose authenticity has been 
questioned, and that its iconography is unusually elaborate compared to securely
provenanced Bronze Age material.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d6",
      "document_summary": "Hudler's 2019 PDF \"No (e)scape?\" discusses Aegean 
glyptic objects of doubtful authenticity, referring to \"gemmae dubitandae\" 
such as the Ring of Nestor. The text emphasizes issues arising when objects lack
secure archaeological properties and contexts, arguing that such uncertainty 
complicates scholarly interpretations and digital representation. The Ring of 
Nestor is cited as an example where archaeological properties either are or will
be under scrutiny, highlighting the ring's problematic status in terms of 
provenance and authenticity.",
      "source_ref": "s6"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d7",
      "document_summary": "The blog post \"Gold Signet Rings of the Minoan and 
Mycenaean Worlds\" on bensozia discusses several famous Aegean gold signet 
rings, including the Ring of Nestor. Citing earlier scholarship, the author 
notes that the Ring of Nestor is a remarkable gold signet-ring known from the 
place of its reported discovery, described as Mycenaean Pylos. The post 
describes its scenes and mentions that the ring's imagery includes ritual or 
mythological elements. The overall tone, however, reflects secondary reporting 
rather than primary archaeological documentation, and does not resolve 
authenticity or provenance debates.",
      "source_ref": "s7"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d8",
      "document_summary": "The Dartmouth Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology site, in
the bibliography for Lesson 24, lists several scholarly works by I. Pini related
to decorated gold finger-rings. One entry is \"The 'Ring of Nestor',\" published
in Oxford Journal of Archaeology 17 (1998) 1–13, followed by references to 
further studies on the same topic. The bibliography confirms that the Ring of 
Nestor has been subject to detailed scholarly treatment in peer-reviewed 
literature, including discussions of its decoration, technological aspects, and 
issues of authenticity and provenance.",
      "source_ref": "s8"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d9",
      "document_summary": "The abstracted ScienceDirect article on the 
technological study of gold objects from four tholos tombs in Messenia 
references Jack L. Davis et al.'s work \"The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin
Warrior of Pylos\" and another work described as \"The ring of Nestor. A glimpse
into the Minoan after-world\" published in London. The context suggests that the
Ring of Nestor has been used in discussions of Minoan conceptions of the 
afterlife and compared to securely excavated Mycenaean and Minoan gold objects 
from tholos tombs, underlining its iconographic significance despite questions 
about its context.",
      "source_ref": "s9"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d10",
      "document_summary": "The Metropolitan Museum of Art Perspectives article 
\"Historic Images of the Greek Bronze Age\" discusses early 20th-century 
archaeological illustration and reconstruction. It mentions two famous gold 
signet rings known as the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor, as well as the 
Phaistos Disk, and notes the role of artist Emile Gilliéron in producing 
drawings and replicas. The article places the Ring of Nestor among iconic but 
controversial artifacts whose interpretation and public image were shaped by 
modern artists and archaeologists rather than by secure archaeological 
excavation records.",
      "source_ref": "s10"
