{"accepted": true, "revision_requested": false, "severity": "clean", "report": 
"# Ring of Nestor\n\n## Ring of Nestor\n\nThe **Ring of Nestor** is a gold 
signet ring widely cited in scholarship on Aegean Bronze Age glyptic art and 
known under this name from early 20th‑century publications on Minoan and 
Mycenaean antiquities. It is described in museum-style and secondary literature 
as a Minoan or Late Minoan object dated to around 1500 BCE and is held in the 
Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, under accession number AN1938.1130. The ring is 
notable for its elaborate ritual imagery, which has been interpreted in relation
to Minoan religion and ideas about the afterlife, and for the modern debates it 
has generated concerning its provenance and authenticity. 
[2][3][4][5][6][8]\n\n## Description\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is described as a 
gold signet ring of Minoan manufacture, assigned on stylistic grounds to around 
1500 BCE (Late Minoan period). It is reported with dimensions of approximately 
2.2 cm in height and 3.9 cm in length (c. 7⁄8 × 1 9⁄16 in.), and is catalogued 
in the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, as AN1938.1130. [2][3]\n\nScholarly summaries 
describe the ring as carrying a complex scene engraved on its bezel, showing 
ritual or divine imagery including a female figure in a boat, altars, sacred 
trees, and baetyls, elements that are characteristic of Minoan ritual 
iconography. [5][9]\n\nComparative studies note that the band of the Ring of 
Nestor is formally similar to that of the so‑called Ring of Minos and to bands 
of gold signet rings from securely excavated early Mycenaean contexts, 
suggesting morphological links between these named rings and excavated Mycenaean
material. [1]\n\n## Discovery and archaeological context\n\nLater accounts 
report that the Ring of Nestor was known from the place of its discovery at 
Mycenaean Pylos, near the so‑called Palace of Nestor. However, methodological 
discussions of Aegean glyptic emphasize that the ring\u0019s specific 
archaeological properties and find context are problematic or unknown, and it is
therefore treated as an object without secure excavation data. [7][6]\n\nA 
popular archaeological summary states that the ring was first described by 
Arthur Evans in 1925 and characterizes it as a gold signet ring from Crete 
dating to the Late Minoan period. This description associates the ring with 
Cretan contexts in modern discourse, even though its original find circumstances
are considered uncertain in later methodological treatments. [3][6]\n\nStudies 
of securely excavated material from Pylos, such as the four Minoan‑style gold 
signet rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor (Late 
Helladic IIA), are frequently used as comparanda when discussing the Ring of 
Nestor, since they provide well-documented Mycenaean contexts for Minoan‑style 
signet rings against which unprovenanced pieces can be evaluated. [1]\n\n## 
Scholarly interpretations and debates\n\nThe Ring of Nestor has been the focus 
of dedicated scholarly analysis. A bibliography of Aegean prehistoric 
