objects such as the Ring of Minos and to the Phaistos Disk, with modern 
scholarship noting that these famous pieces were historically promoted in part 
through early 20th‑century reproductions and displays. In this perspective, the 
Ring of Nestor forms part of a group of high-profile Aegean Bronze Age objects 
whose modern reception history intersects with questions of authenticity, 
